  "lyrics": [
    {
      "start": 0.05,
      "end": 2.402,
      "text": "Numbers in tables are not one type.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 2.402,
      "end": 3.763,
      "text": "Integers count.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 3.763,
      "end": 7.416,
      "text": "Decimals measure money and other exact fractions.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 7.416,
      "end": 10.194,
      "text": "Floats approximate scientific values.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 10.194,
      "end": 12.666,
      "text": "If you mix them, you will round a dollar.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 12.666,
      "end": 17.513,
      "text": "INTEGER, INT, BIGINT are whole numbers of different ranges.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 17.513,
      "end": 20.375,
      "text": "customer_id is an integer.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 20.375,
      "end": 23.43,
      "text": "quantity is an integer if you sell whole books.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 23.43,
      "end": 25.472,
      "text": "Do not use a float for a count.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 25.472,
      "end": 29.722,
      "text": "DECIMAL p comma s stores exact decimal digits.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 29.722,
      "end": 31.402,
      "text": "p is total digits.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 31.402,
      "end": 33.472,
      "text": "s is digits after the point.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 33.472,
      "end": 36.916,
      "text": "DECIMAL ten comma two is a common money shape.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 36.916,
      "end": 40.361,
      "text": "Forty-two dollars and fifteen cents is exact.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 40.361,
      "end": 44.027,
      "text": "NUMERIC is often the same family as DECIMAL.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 44.027,
      "end": 47.694,
      "text": "REAL and DOUBLE PRECISION are binary floating point.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 47.694,
      "end": 52.125,
      "text": "They are fast, and they cannot represent one tenth exactly.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 52.125,
      "end": 54.68,
      "text": "Summing a column of floats can drift.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 54.68,
      "end": 57.25,
      "text": "That drift is unacceptable for money.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 57.25,
      "end": 59.5,
      "text": "It may be fine for a sensor reading.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 59.5,
      "end": 62.194,
      "text": "SQLite has a softer type system.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 62.194,
      "end": 66.763,
      "text": "A column declared INTEGER can still hold text unless you constrain it.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 66.763,
      "end": 68.736,
      "text": "Affinity is not a CHECK.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 68.736,
      "end": 73.638,
      "text": "If you need integer only, say so with a constraint or a stricter engine.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 73.638,
      "end": 77.541,
      "text": "Auto incrementing integers make convenient surrogates.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 77.541,
      "end": 78.93,
      "text": "They are not magic.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 78.93,
      "end": 81.583,
      "text": "Gaps appear after a rolled-back insert.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 81.583,
      "end": 83.847,
      "text": "Do not treat them as row counts.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 83.847,
      "end": 88.277,
      "text": "BOOLEAN is a number in some dialects and a real type in others.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 88.277,
      "end": 91.277,
      "text": "Store it as a real boolean when you can.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 91.277,
      "end": 94.458,
      "text": "Or as a constrained integer zero or one.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 94.458,
      "end": 97.43,
      "text": "Do not store yes as text if you mean a flag.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 97.43,
      "end": 99.555,
      "text": "Overflow is a real error.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 99.555,
      "end": 101.569,
      "text": "TINYINT cannot hold a million.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 101.569,
      "end": 105.736,
      "text": "Pick a range with room, not a range that matches today's sample.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 105.736,
      "end": 107.111,
      "text": "Write the sentence.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 107.111,
      "end": 113.375,
      "text": "This column is a count of books, whole, never negative, fits in a normal integer.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 113.375,
      "end": 118.208,
      "text": "This column is a price in dollars, exact cents, never a float.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 118.208,
      "end": 120.125,
      "text": "Then the type is obvious.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 120.125,
      "end": 123.208,
      "text": "Money in integer cents is a valid design.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 123.208,
      "end": 127.569,
      "text": "total_cents INTEGER, and you format dollars at the edge.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 127.569,
      "end": 130.222,
      "text": "DECIMAL is the other valid design.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 130.222,
      "end": 134.458,
      "text": "Mixing them in one table is a third, invalid design.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 134.458,
      "end": 137.083,
      "text": "Pick one and convert at the boundary.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 137.083,
      "end": 139.444,
      "text": "Percentages can be DECIMAL too.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 139.444,
      "end": 143.0,
      "text": "A float percent will surprise you in a tax calculation.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 143.0,
      "end": 148.194,
      "text": "Scientific measurements can stay FLOAT if the instrument was already approximate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 148.194,
      "end": 151.166,
      "text": "Do not fake precision the sensor never had.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 151.166,
      "end": 154.93,
      "text": "SERIAL and IDENTITY are integer generators.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 154.93,
      "end": 156.694,
      "text": "They are not a type of quantity.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 156.694,
      "end": 158.375,
      "text": "Do not use them on total.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 158.375,
      "end": 161.375,
      "text": "Unsigned integers exist in some engines.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 161.375,
      "end": 165.208,
      "text": "They forbid negatives, which a CHECK can also do more clearly.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 165.208,
      "end": 169.194,
      "text": "Prefer a signed integer plus CHECK if you want portable SQL.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 169.194,
      "end": 172.222,
      "text": "Division in integer types truncates.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 172.222,
      "end": 176.597,
      "text": "quantity divided by two may be a whole number you did not mean.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 176.597,
      "end": 180.722,
      "text": "If you need a rate, use DECIMAL and say how many places.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 180.722,
      "end": 183.875,
      "text": "NaN and infinity can appear in floats.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 183.875,
      "end": 185.861,
      "text": "They should never appear in money.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 185.861,
      "end": 189.597,
      "text": "The family you chose at the start forbids them, or should.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 189.597,
      "end": 198.263,
      "text": "Write examples in the comment: quantity 2, total 42.00, tax_rate 0.08.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 198.263,
      "end": 202.611,
      "text": "If an example needs a float, you picked the float family on purpose.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 202.611,
      "end": 204.861,
      "text": "Counts, money, science.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 204.861,
      "end": 206.013,
      "text": "Three homes.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 206.013,
      "end": 207.777,
      "text": "Put each column in one.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 207.777,
      "end": 211.916,
      "text": "If a spreadsheet formatted it as currency, it is not a float.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 211.916,
      "end": 214.472,
      "text": "If a sensor emitted it, it might be.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 214.472,
      "end": 217.43,
      "text": "If a person counted it, it is an integer.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 217.43,
      "end": 220.75,
      "text": "Say those three sentences when a column is born.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 220.75,
      "end": 224.916,
      "text": "Then DECIMAL, FLOAT, or INTEGER is not a debate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 224.916,
      "end": 226.569,
      "text": "It is a classification.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 226.569,
      "end": 229.597,
      "text": "Misclassification is how pennies vanish.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 229.597,
      "end": 234.041,
      "text": "Pennies vanishing is how finance stops trusting the database.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 234.041,
      "end": 236.527,
      "text": "Keep their trust with exact types.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 236.527,
      "end": 240.972,
      "text": "Keep science honest with approximate types that admit they are approximate.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 240.972,
      "end": 242.263,
      "text": "Keep counts whole.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 242.263,
      "end": 244.236,
      "text": "The rest is dialect spelling.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 244.236,
      "end": 250.18,
      "text": "Whole numbers, approximate fractions, and exact decimals are three different jobs.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 250.18,
      "end": 258.069,
      "text": "Integers count things: quantities, ids you generated, flags stored as zero and one if you must.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 258.069,
      "end": 260.486,
      "text": "Signed integers allow negatives.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 260.486,
      "end": 263.847,
      "text": "Unsigned, where the engine has them, do not.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 263.847,
      "end": 268.194,
      "text": "TINYINT through BIGINT are range costumes around the same idea.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 268.194,
      "end": 270.18,
      "text": "Do not memorize the ranges.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 270.18,
      "end": 276.888,
      "text": "Look them up when a column is born, and pick the smallest range that cannot overflow in real life.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 276.888,
      "end": 282.583,
      "text": "Overflow is a silent disaster if the engine wraps, or a loud one if it errors.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 282.583,
      "end": 283.722,
      "text": "Prefer loud.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 283.722,
      "end": 287.069,
      "text": "FLOAT and DOUBLE are binary approximations.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 287.186,
      "end": 293.149,
      "text": "They are correct for scientific measurements where 0.1 repeating is a known compromise.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 293.149,
      "end": 294.719,
      "text": "They are wrong for money.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 294.719,
      "end": 302.816,
      "text": "The Vancouver stock index story is the parable: tiny errors, added every trade, became a crash in trust.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 302.816,
      "end": 308.261,
      "text": "If you add a price to a price in FLOAT, you can get a value that is not any real cent.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 308.261,
      "end": 315.663,
      "text": "DECIMAL, also called NUMERIC, stores exact base-ten values with a declared precision and scale.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 315.663,
      "end": 323.024,
      "text": "DECIMAL eight comma two means eight digits total, two after the point: a money column with room to breathe.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 323.024,
      "end": 329.011,
      "text": "Money, interest rates you must audit, and anything a receipt will print belong in DECIMAL.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 329.011,
      "end": 333.774,
      "text": "Percentages you will multiply back into money belong in DECIMAL too.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 333.774,
      "end": 340.941,
      "text": "Sensor readings, graphics, and statistics that already live in floating point may honestly be FLOAT.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 340.941,
      "end": 343.483,
      "text": "Say approximate in the comment if you do.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 343.483,
      "end": 346.899,
      "text": "Do not use FLOAT because it felt like the default number.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 346.899,
      "end": 348.622,
      "text": "There is no default number.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 348.622,
      "end": 354.302,
      "text": "There is a classification: counted, exact fraction, approximate fraction.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 354.302,
      "end": 358.441,
      "text": "If a spreadsheet formatted it as currency, it is not a float.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 358.441,
      "end": 361.399,
      "text": "If a person counted it, it is an integer.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 361.399,
      "end": 364.761,
      "text": "If a lab instrument emitted it, it might be a float.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
      "start": 364.761,
      "end": 367.788,
      "text": "Misclassification is how pennies vanish.",
      "slide": ""
    },
    {
